  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of **examples of preventive medicine practices**, organized by type:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfe5 What Is Preventive Medicine?\n\nPreventive medicine is a holistic approach to healthcare that considers the whole patient and the myriad factors that influence their well-being. This medical specialty differs from traditional medicine in that its focus is **proactive rather than reactive**.\n\nThere are **3 main types** of preventive medicine: primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Primary Prevention\n*(Preventing disease before it occurs)*\n\nPrimary prevention can be defined as an action or behavior that does not allow a disease or adverse event to occur. Examples include **immunization, smoking cessation**, or initiation of an **exercise program** with the goal of disease prevention.\n\nKey examples include:\n\n- **Vaccinations:** Vaccinations represent perhaps the single most effective preventive care measure in medical history. They have eradicated or dramatically reduced many diseases that once caused widespread suffering and death. Vaccines are important in building a child's immune system. Children must have shots against diseases like **mumps and polio**.\n\n- **Lifestyle Counseling:** Clinical preventive medicine physicians may provide **counseling for unhealthy habits**, run preventive health screenings, and administer immunizations. These practitioners may work with patients who would benefit from lifestyle changes, often encountering cases involving diabetes, smoking, or obesity.\n\n- **STI Prevention:** All STIs can be prevented, all can be effectively managed, and many can be cured. Due to new STI screening techniques and technologies, healthcare professionals are better equipped than ever to help prevent the spread of STIs. Anyone who is sexually active should be periodically screened for STIs.\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Secondary Prevention\n*(Early detection of disease in asymptomatic stages)*\n\nSecondary preventive medicine focuses on the **early detection** of illness. This gives the patient a better chance of getting treatment. Detecting the ailment early slows down its progression.\n\nKey examples include:\n\n- **Cancer Screenings:** Advances in cancer detection led to improved screening for various malignancies; examples include the widespread use of **colonoscopy** in colorectal cancer screening, **mammography** in breast cancer screening, and **Pap smear and HPV testing** in cervical cancer.\n\n- **Cardiovascular Screenings:** Screenings for **elevated cholesterol levels** and **hypertension** serve critical roles in preventing, identifying, and controlling cardiovascular diseases.\n\n- **Newborn Screening:** Examples of treatments under this category also include **newborn screening** and cancer screening.\n\n- **Blood Pressure & Smoking Screening:** Physicians commonly perform preventive practices such as **blood pressure, weight and height measurements**, screening for dyslipidemia, **screening for alcohol use**, screening for smoking (and brief advice for smokers), colon and prostate cancer screening, and **influenza immunization**.\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Tertiary Prevention\n*(Minimizing the impact of an existing disease)*\n\nTertiary preventive medicine aims to **limit the damage** of the current illness. Making the patient as comfortable as possible is part of this level of care. Future care and long-term treatment modalities can help the patient achieve health stability, helping the patient return to daily routines.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udca1 Why Is Preventive Medicine Important?\n\nPracticing preventive medicine can lower costs, as 75% of the country's annual health spending goes toward chronic and largely preventable diseases. Heart disease and stroke are responsible for one-third of deaths in the US each year, costing the healthcare system approximately **$216 billion annually** while also resulting in **$147 billion in lost workplace productivity**.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, preventive medicine encompasses a wide range of practices \u2014 from **vaccinations and screenings** to **lifestyle counseling and long-term disease management** \u2014 all aimed at keeping people healthier and reducing the overall burden on the healthcare system.",
